ds of practical ways we  can do that  but also we've got to take on the forces  that are doing this to us at the moment  it's a bit like someone is constantly  pouring itching powder into our heads  and then they're leaning forward and  saying you know um you might want to  learn how to meditate then you wouldn't  scratch so much right of course  meditation has real value i'm in favor  of it but we've also got to stop the  people who are pouring the itching  powder on us all the time and one of  them is exactly what you're saying  constant interruptions that degrade our  ability to ever get into a state of deep  focus  and this these constant interruptions  they actually cause cognitive  degradation we are actually  rotting our brains you've concluded  well it was really chilling i went to  interview one of the leading  neuroscientists in the world a man named  professor earl miller who's at mit the  massachusetts institute of technology  and that was the phrase he used to me  that we are living in what he called a  perfect storm of cognitive degradation  there were so many scientists it was  because i didn't realize how big this  was um i could feel it in myself i could  see it in the kids that i love i didn't  realize how  how  deep this crisis goes until i went to  interview so many scientists who said  similar things like that the leading  expert on children's attention problems  in the united states professor joel nigg  said to me  that we are living in what he called an  attentional pathogenic environment it's  one where it's getting harder for almost  everyone to to focus and pay attention  but the really important thing to know  is there are solutions when we  understand the  real range of things that happening to  us and the factors degrading our  attention range really widely from the  food we eat to the hours we don't sleep  once we understand these causes we can  begin to deal with them at these two  levels and it requires us to change the  way we think 